  ? UNCERTAIN: Uninitialized proxy can be claimed by an attacker
    Note: Upon reviewing the StakingModule.initialize function in 
contracts/StakingModule.sol, I confirmed that initialization is externally 
callable and protected only by the OpenZeppelin initializer modifier. On its 
first successful invocation, it assigns DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE to msg.sender via 
_setupRole, while also setting the tel address, so an attacker would become 
administrator if an upgradeable proxy were deployed without initialization data 
or otherwise left uninitialized. The initializer modifier prevents repeated 
initialization but does not restrict the first caller, and no deployment-time or
trusted-controller safeguard is present in this contract. However, the provided 
source does not include the proxy contract, deployment scripts, constructor, or 
initialization transaction, so it cannot establish whether the affected proxy is
actually initialized atomically during deployment. The implementation contract 
itself being callable is not necessarily exploitable against the proxy because 
implementation and proxy storage are distinct. Therefore, the hypothesis is 
plausible but cannot be conclusively confirmed without verifying the deployment 
path and proxy initialization state.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Unvalidated plugin registration enables global denial of ser
    Justification: Upon reviewing the StakingModule plugin-management and 
callback paths, I confirmed that a PLUGIN_EDITOR_ROLE holder can register an 
arbitrary address without a nonzero-code check, interface validation, or 
plugin-count bound. The addPlugin function only checks pluginsMapping, after 
which the address is iterated by claimable, claimableAt, totalSupply, _claim, 
and _notifyStakeChangeAllPlugins. The requiresNotification guard is commented 
out, so every registered address receives notifyStakeChange during stake, exit, 
partial exit, and slash-related flows. A malicious contract can therefore 
deliberately revert in notifyStakeChange or any view/claim callback, causing 
affected operations to revert for all users. An EOA or incompatible contract can
also cause ABI decoding failures in functions expected to return values such as 
claimable or totalClaimable. Although removePlugin allows an authorized operator
to recover by removing the address, it does not prevent the denial of service 
while the plugin remains registered, and pausing does not make view calls 
succeed. SimplePlugin itself safely implements a no-op notification, but that 
does not constrain other registered addresses.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Removing a plugin can permanently strand user rewards
    Justification: Upon reviewing StakingModule.removePlugin, claim, 
claimFromIndividualPlugin, claimable, and SimplePlugin.claim, I confirmed that 
plugin removal can strand outstanding rewards. removePlugin removes the plugin 
from the active plugins array and sets pluginsMapping to false without checking 
totalClaimable, settling user balances, or preserving a claim route. All 
user-facing claim paths iterate only over the current active plugins, and 
SimplePlugin.claim is protected by onlyStaking, requiring the StakingModule as 
msg.sender; therefore users cannot call the removed plugin directly. The plugin 
retains each user’s checkpoints and _totalOwed, and its TEL balance remains 
reserved, but those assets are inaccessible through normal user functionality 
after removal. The PLUGIN_EDITOR_ROLE can technically restore access by calling 
addPlugin with the same address, so permanent loss is not mathematically 
inevitable from this code alone; however, users have no independent recovery 
mechanism if administrators decline to re-add it or the plugin is operationally 
deprecated. claimAndExitFor does not mitigate this because it also iterates only
active plugins and is restricted to RECOVERY_ROLE. Thus, the core 
reward-stranding vulnerability is real, although the hypothesis's claim of 
irreversible loss is conditional.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Stake accounting can exceed actual TEL received for fee-on-t
    Justification: Upon reviewing StakingModule.initialize, _stake, _exit, and 
rescueTokens, I confirmed a conditional accounting vulnerability for 
fee-on-transfer or otherwise nonstandard TEL tokens. initialize assigns 
_telAddress directly to tel without validating its implementation or transfer 
semantics. In _stake, the requested amount is added to the user checkpoint and 
_totalStaked before safeTransferFrom executes, with no pre- and post-transfer 
balance comparison; therefore, if the token credits fewer tokens than requested,
the contract records an overstated liability. _exit subsequently attempts to 
transfer the full recorded amount, which may revert when the contract lacks 
sufficient balance, or may consume other users’ funds if sufficient surplus 
exists. rescueTokens also assumes _totalStaked is backed by the token balance 
and can underflow when the balance is lower, potentially preventing recovery 
operations. SafeERC20 only validates call success and does not ensure the exact 
amount was received. The issue is conditional on deployment with an incompatible
TEL token or an intentionally nonstandard token, but no source-level guard 
prevents that configuration.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: FeeBuyback credits all residual TEL balance to the current r
    Justification: Upon reviewing FeeBuyback.submit in 
contracts/fee-buyback/FeeBuyback.sol, I confirmed the alleged asset-loss 
vulnerability. In the direct-TEL branch, the contract transfers only amount from
_safe, but then approves and credits _telcoin.balanceOf(address(this)) to 
recipient through _referral.increaseClaimableBy. Consequently, any TEL already 
held by FeeBuyback is included in the recipient's reward. The aggregator branch 
has the same defect: after the swap, it uses the contract's entire current TEL 
balance rather than measuring the balance increase attributable to that swap. 
SimplePlugin.increaseClaimableBy then records the supplied amount as claimable 
and pulls exactly that amount from FeeBuyback using transferFrom, so the 
residual balance is transferred into the plugin and assigned to the chosen 
recipient. There is no before/after balance accounting, output validation, or 
guard excluding pre-existing TEL. Although submit is restricted by onlyOwner, 
that is an authorization boundary rather than protection against a malicious, 
compromised, or improperly acting owner, and the owner can select an arbitrary 
recipient. The hypothesis is therefore directly supported by the implementation.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: FeeBuyback ignores ERC20 transfer and approval return values
    Justification: Upon reviewing FeeBuyback.submit in 
contracts/fee-buyback/FeeBuyback.sol, I confirmed the unchecked ERC20 
return-value vulnerability. In both the TEL branch and the general ERC20 branch,
transferFrom and approve are invoked directly and their boolean results are 
discarded. An ERC20 token can therefore return false without reverting, after 
which execution continues as though funding and allowance setup succeeded. The 
TEL branch is particularly problematic: it credits _referral with the contract’s
entire current TEL balance, not the balance increase attributable to this 
submission, so a failed transferFrom can cause pre-existing TEL to be assigned 
to the requested recipient. The general branch similarly proceeds to the 
aggregator call after a failed transferFrom or approve, potentially operating on
existing balances or failing later depending on swap behavior. onlyOwner 
restricts who can invoke submit, but does not make configured tokens trustworthy
or prevent accounting errors caused by false-return tokens. rescueERC20 also 
ignores transfer results, although it is separate from the stated path. 
SafeERC20 checks and balance-delta accounting are therefore warranted.
  ✗ REJECTED: RECOVERY_ROLE can drain TEL reserved for user rewards
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged asset-loss issue in 
StakingModule.rescueTokens and the reward accounting in SimplePlugin, I 
determined this is a false positive for the supplied implementation. 
StakingModule.rescueTokens protects the module’s TEL balance by subtracting 
_totalStaked, but SimplePlugin’s prefunded reward TEL is held by the plugin 
contract, not by the staking module, and is separately protected by 
SimplePlugin.rescueTokens using _totalOwed. A normal claim invokes 
SimplePlugin.claim, which transfers rewards directly to the user, so those 
liabilities never become rescuable TEL in StakingModule. In _claimAndExit, 
rewards are intentionally transferred to the module and incorporated into the 
user’s new stake; _totalStaked is increased by the claimed amount before the 
module transfers the requested amount, preserving the corresponding liability. 
The recovery role therefore cannot use StakingModule.rescueTokens to drain 
SimplePlugin’s claimable balances. Although arbitrary TEL donations or other 
plugin-specific designs could require separate review, the shown SimplePlugin 
architecture does not establish the claimed exploitable path.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: RECOVERY_ROLE can redirect users’ migrated principal and rew
    Justification: Upon reviewing StakingModule.claimAndExitFor, _claim, and 
_exit, I confirmed that a holder of RECOVERY_ROLE can redirect both a user’s 
claimable rewards and staked TEL to an arbitrary address. claimAndExitFor 
accepts independent account and to parameters, invokes _claim(account, to, 
auxData), and then _exit(account, to); neither internal function authenticates 
the user, validates the destination, or requires migration authorization. _claim
causes each plugin to send rewards directly to to, while _exit transfers the 
entire recorded stake to the same address after clearing the account’s 
checkpoint. The whenPaused modifier and nonReentrant guard do not prevent misuse
once the contract is paused; importantly, RECOVERY_ROLE itself cannot pause the 
contract, so exploitation by that role alone requires an already-paused state or
additional PAUSER_ROLE privilege. Nevertheless, the code provides no consent or 
cryptographic proof tying the migration to the affected account, making 
arbitrary redirection possible for a compromised or misused recovery operator. 
This is a privileged-role abuse issue rather than an unprivileged exploit.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Owner-controlled arbitrary call enables FeeBuyback asset and
    Justification: Upon reviewing FeeBuyback.submit in 
contracts/fee-buyback/FeeBuyback.sol, I confirmed that the contract grants an 
unrestricted arbitrary-call capability to any address recognized by onlyOwner().
The function accepts caller-controlled wallet and walletData parameters and 
executes wallet.call{value: 0}(walletData) without validating the target, 
function selector, or calldata contents. Consequently, an authorized owner can 
set wallet to an ERC20 token contract and provide transfer(address,uint256) 
calldata to move tokens held by FeeBuyback, or provide approve(address,uint256) 
calldata to grant an attacker-controlled spender an allowance. The call executes
with FeeBuyback as msg.sender, so token contracts will treat the FeeBuyback 
balance and approvals as the affected assets. The subsequent token, recipient, 
and amount checks do not prevent this because the arbitrary call occurs first 
and execution can return early when those values are zero. Although onlyOwner 
restricts exploitation to the owner or a compromised owner key, it does not 
constrain the operation to the intended primary wallet transaction. No target or
selector allowlist exists, so the reported access-control issue is real.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Successful aggregator call is accepted without verifying TEL
    Justification: Upon reviewing FeeBuyback.submit in 
contracts/fee-buyback/FeeBuyback.sol, I confirmed that the alleged 
missing-output validation exists and can result in asset loss. For non-TEL 
tokens, the function first calls transferFrom(_safe, address(this), amount), 
approves the immutable _aggregator, and then executes _aggregator.call{value: 
msg.value}(swapData). The only swap validation is require(swapResult, 
"FeeBuyback: swap transaction failed"); there is no check of the TEL balance 
before and after the call, no minimum-output parameter, and no verification that
the expected source amount was consumed through an approved route. Consequently,
an aggregator invocation can return success while producing zero or insufficient
TEL, leaving the safe’s source tokens spent without obtaining the intended 
reward asset. The subsequent approval and _referral.increaseClaimableBy call use
the contract’s entire TEL balance, which does not establish that the current 
swap produced any TEL and may even credit zero or residual TEL. Although submit 
is restricted by onlyOwner, compromise, misconfiguration, or malicious behavior 
of the authorized service/aggregator still provides a clear exploitable path.


